\paragraph{70B-class DocRED results.}
\label{app:70b-docred}

Table~\ref{tab:70b-docred-ablation} presents available 70B LoRA configurations on DocRED (998 documents).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.6pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l rrrr rr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode}
  & \textbf{DR\,$\downarrow$} & \textbf{RV\,$\downarrow$} & \textbf{ET\,$\downarrow$} & \textbf{CF}
  & \textbf{R-F1\,$\uparrow$} & \textbf{E-F1\,$\uparrow$} \\
\midrule
Unconstrained & --- & --- & --- & ---  & 43.80 & --- \\
S1            & \textbf{0} & 0 & 0 & --- & 43.95 & --- \\
Beam4 S1      & \textbf{0} & 0 & 0 & \textbf{0} & \textbf{48.14} & \textbf{85.57} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{70B-class (Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (AWQ INT4)) LoRA results on DocRED (998 docs).
CF: violation count. F1 $\times$100. ---: pending full ablation data.}
\label{tab:70b-docred-ablation}
\end{table}

Beam search produces a substantially larger R-F1 gain on DocRED ($+$4.19\,pp) than SciERC ($+$0.28\,pp), suggesting that the benefit scales with schema complexity (96 vs.\ 7 relations); parse failures also drop from 12.5\% (greedy) to 0.5\% (beam), indicating that beam exploration helps navigate tighter constraint spaces.

